mdl 1;
/*
 * Demo users, split out of 06 when neither file was re-runnable: `create module
 * role` had no `or modify` form, so re-executing 06 stopped at the first
 * existing role, and `CREATE DEMO USER` had none either, so this file halted on
 * "demo user already exists". Both forms exist now — 06 was migrated to
 * `create or modify module role`, and the two statements below use
 * `CREATE OR MODIFY DEMO USER`, which reports "Unchanged demo user" on a second
 * run and writes nothing (verified in FINDINGS §92). So the split is no longer
 * load-bearing and neither file needs `--continue-on-error`.
 */

/*
 * The cost of hashing those two passwords, which is most of what this service
 * spends its time on.
 *
 * A consumed OData service authenticates with basic auth on every request and
 * holds no session, so each call is a full login — and BCrypt is deliberately
 * slow. Measured against this backend, one `GET /odata/f1/Drivers?$top=20`:
 *
 *   cost 12 (Mendix default)        ~360 ms      the hash is 88% of the request
 *   cost 12, *wrong* password       ~346 ms      same cost; it hashes either way
 *   cost 8                           ~73 ms
 *   no credentials at all            ~42 ms      the floor, no hash computed
 *
 * §31 traced the same thing from the other end: 303 ms of a 500 ms page turn on
 * the live service, 301 ms of a 370 ms page turn on the cached one. Optimising
 * the SQL underneath would be optimising 14%.
 *
 * Cost 8 rather than 12 is a judgement about *these* accounts, not about
 * password hashing in general. Both are machine accounts with generated
 * 20-character passwords, reachable only from the frontend app; there is no
 * human password in this database and no login page in front of it. The human
 * user lives in Formula1Frontend, which keeps the default 12 — the setting is
 * per app, so the two do not have to agree.
 *
 * The cost is baked into each stored hash, so this only applies to passwords
 * hashed after it changes. It takes effect here because the demo users below
 * are recreated at startup; on a real deployment existing users keep their old
 * cost until their next password change.
 *
 *
 * THE DOWNGRADE IS GONE
 *
 * `ALTER SETTINGS MODEL BcryptCost = 8;` used to be the line under this
 * comment. It shrank the cost rather than removing it, and shrinking a work
 * factor is a deliberate weakening — defensible only because these are machine
 * accounts, and taken only because the real fix could not be written.
 *
 * The real fix is custom authentication: no password is presented per request,
 * so no hash is computed and the work factor stops mattering. It needs
 * `authentication microflow Module.X` on the service, which MDL accepted while
 * silently dropping the microflow name — the build then failed CE0333 "Please
 * select a microflow to use for authentication". mxcli `715bac5` carries the
 * name through, and `mxcli check` gained MDL-ODATA04 for the unnamed form. All
 * five services authenticate through Formula1Backend.AuthenticateApiClient now
 * (16-ops-procedures.mdl), the frontend's clients send the key as a header
 * instead of a password, and the cost is back at the Mendix default of 12.
 * FINDINGS §40, §48.
 */

/* A demo user per role, so both services can be exercised without a login page. */
CREATE OR MODIFY DEMO USER 'f1api' ( Password: 'F1apiConsumer!2345', UserRoles: (ApiConsumer) );
CREATE OR MODIFY DEMO USER 'f1admin' ( Password: 'F1adminAccess!2345', UserRoles: (Administrator) );
